WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.480
Hey everyone, Jared here, and today we’re
talking about the blackhole that devoured

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:09.200
weeks of my life: Fallout 4.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:13.080
Like many of its predecessors, Fallout 4 follows
a vault dweller bravely traversing

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:14.940
the post-apocalyptic Wasteland.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:19.520
Unlike Fallout 3, which features a vault dweller
in search of his father, Fallout 4 chronicles

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:24.560
a weird inverted Oedipal scenario where you
kill your son and destroy his legacy.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:29.330
All Fallout games feature super mutants, Deathclaws,
and feral ghouls menacing the human race.

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:33.249
But in Fallout 4, there’s a new danger:
synthetic humans.

00:00:33.249 --> 00:00:37.629
And while the perils of the Wasteland still
pose an existential threat to humankind, Fallout

00:00:37.629 --> 00:00:43.070
4 introduces a new, purely philosophical threat
that is: What does it mean to be human?

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:47.940
Through the synths, the game makes us question
everything we know about our very humanity.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:51.699
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on The Philosophy
of Fallout 4.

00:00:51.699 --> 00:00:56.260
The synth-creating Institute serves as the
primary antagonist to the rest of the Commonwealth.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:01:00.569
The Institute was built when the surviving
scientists of a fictionalized MIT, known as

00:01:00.569 --> 00:01:06.000
CIT, dug themselves underground to shield
themselves from the ravages of the irradiated surface.

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:09.680
Dedicating themselves to research and the
advancement of humanity, they quickly scrapped

00:01:09.689 --> 00:01:14.229
their plans to improve life on the surface,
and instead opted to create serene waterfalls

00:01:14.229 --> 00:01:16.950
and creepy synthetic children.

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:20.939
The Institute’s synth program draws suspicion
from the inhabitants of the Wasteland, who

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:25.940
believe - correctly - that the Institute is
replacing their loved ones with synthetic counterparts.

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:32.780
It also sets them at odds with the Railroad,
who believe the synths are sentient and being

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:37.190
exploited by the Institute, and the Brotherhood
of Steel, who feel the synthetic humanoids

00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:40.000
are another case of technology gone too far.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.100
The fundamental question about synths, and
our relation to them, falls into two camps.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:49.549
There’s “humanism,” a philosophy centered
on, surprise, the “human,” and “posthumanism,”

00:01:49.549 --> 00:01:53.000
the deconstruction and decentering of what
it means to be human.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:57.350
As the player, one choice faced in the game
is to believe, as Father does, that the synths

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:58.719
are mere robots.

00:01:58.719 --> 00:02:02.070
Or you can believe, as the Railroad does,
that because they’ve developed sentience

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:03.880
they’re deserving of rights.

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:08.260
We could see the synth storyline as an uninspired,
lazy, cliche about discrimination,

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:15.860
but it also does a great job of exploring the line
between human and non-human.

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:19.980
Nowhere is this divide explored more explicitly
than in the ideology of the Brotherhood of

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:22.720
Steel and their opposition to the Institute.

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:25.960
The Brotherhood are, for lack of a better
term, synth racists;

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:27.420
"Shoot to kill!"

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:30.720
they believe the cybernetic organisms are an abomination to nature.

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:40.660
And it’s not just the fear of them being
used by the Institute to spy on society, even

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:44.780
the rebellious synths shepherded to freedom
by the Railroad are targets of Maxson’s soldiers.

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:52.880
Walking around Brotherhood territory with
Valentine, an early-version synth, subjects

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:55.280
you to a deeply unsettling level of harassment.

00:02:57.269 --> 00:03:01.220
The Brotherhood ride around on the “Prydwen”
led by a guy named Arthur - a reference to

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:03.830
the ship and hero of the tales of King Arthur.

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:09.150
Their ranks emulate medieval Europe, with
titles like Knight, Paladin, Scribe and Squires.

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:13.120
This is perhaps the best indication of how
they perceive themselves: chivalrous knights

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:17.750
out to battle monsters and protect the helpless
remains of humanity and its purity.

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:20.310
The Brotherhood adopts a kind of classical
morality.

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:25.360
They believe in “the human” as a pure,
inherently valuable entity worth defending.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:29.319
Conceived in a womb of a loving mother, au
natural, you get the idea

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:31.480
They can be roughly described as “humanist.”

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:34.920
Now, there’s a ton of different kinds of
humanisms who all believe different things,

00:03:34.939 --> 00:03:38.910
but we’re really just using the term “humanist”
to stress a level of assumptions they share

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:43.560
with other humanists: that there is a basic
human nature, and that the human, as opposed

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:47.000
to the inhuman, are the primary subjects of
the world.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:51.439
If the Brotherhood is so bothered by synths
- it’s because this amalgam of man and machine

00:03:51.439 --> 00:03:56.119
threatens the very identity that defines them
and the humanity they seek to defend.

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:05.640
Long before the Brotherhood, humanity was
obsessed with defining themselves by what

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:06.640
they are not.

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:11.470
We’re not like animals because we use tools,
think abstractly, and use emojis.

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:16.359
Back in the days of Ancient Greece, this anxiety
mainly manifested itself in interspecies creatures

00:04:16.359 --> 00:04:18.280
like centaurs and minotaurs.

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:22.970
The combination of species disrupted the traditional
boundaries of Greek society - between man

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:25.390
and animal, polis and nature, etc.

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:29.270
Today, that anxiety about the boundaries of
the “human” creeps into our relationship

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:30.580
with technology.

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:36.170
Instead of human vs animal, it’s more like
human vs animals/machines/microbes and holy

00:04:36.170 --> 00:04:37.919
shit the world is scary stuff.

00:04:37.919 --> 00:04:42.360
A quick survey of the scifi genre will reveal
a ton of movies scared shitless about human

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:46.500
genetic manipulation, machines overtaking
humans, machines f*cking humans.

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:47.580
You get the idea.

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:51.960
In her essay “The Cyborg Manifesto,” philosopher
Donna Haraway investigates our construction

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:56.310
of “the human” and how this chasm, between
“organism and machine,” is always enforced

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:58.020
by a kind of “border war.”

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:02.960
We defend the figure of the human against
degradations, impurities, infections, and so on.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:03.460
 

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:12.500
It shouldn’t be surprising to learn that
back in the day, people argued dialysis that was

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:17.169
an unsavory attempt to play God, an unholy
marriage between man and machine.

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:19.940
Life-saving technologies are often considered
undignified.

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:21.040
"Is he still alright?"

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:23.820
'Oh, no, no, he has health problems."

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:24.420
"Uh huh."

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:28.820
In order to preserve their idea of a pure
humanity, the Brotherhood wants to exterminate

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:31.370
the synths, who threaten these boundaries.

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:35.590
The Brotherhood’s defense of “the human”
isn’t all that new, and their response to

00:05:35.590 --> 00:05:37.130
synths isn’t too surprising.

00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:42.550
They’re essentially railing against miscegenation,
or the mixing of races, using the same invocations

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:46.720
of purity and nature that have always worked
so well when being dicks to entire groups

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:47.720
of people.

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:52.230
However, the Brotherhood’s idea of “human”
is deeply flawed, and full of contradictions.

00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:55.910
It’s here that Fallout gets pretty smart,
even if inadvertently.

00:05:55.910 --> 00:06:00.110
The game, through the Brotherhood of Steel,
shows the distinction between human and inhuman

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:01.740
isn’t so black and white,

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:03.320
"I don't recognize you."

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:08.040
and calls into question several myths surrounding the very idea of “the human”.

00:06:08.050 --> 00:06:12.069
The first myth the Brotherhood runs into is
about the purity of the human body - shunning

00:06:12.069 --> 00:06:15.210
the mixture of flesh and machine as we discussed
earlier.

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:17.569
The problem is -- they’re literally cyborgs.

00:06:17.569 --> 00:06:21.630
If you want to rail against the posthuman,
let’s talk about your suits of power armor

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:23.360
that you wear around everywhere.

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:27.979
The power armor enhances the human body like
any implant could - it can augment your vision,

00:06:27.979 --> 00:06:28.979
your strength.

00:06:28.979 --> 00:06:32.830
For Proctor Ingram, it even fills the void
of the missing legs she lost in battle.

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:37.389
For Haraway, this boundary is always a fiction,
and the biggest enforcers of the border between

00:06:37.389 --> 00:06:41.520
human and inhuman are gleefully ignorant of
how porous that border is.

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:46.720
Those things that make us human - using tools,
language, etc., are seen in both apes and birds.

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:49.400
We’re also molded, according to Haraway,
by the environment.

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:58.220
In real life, the “pure” human body is
full of foreign DNA and bacteria.

00:06:58.220 --> 00:07:02.680
Likewise, the radiation wreaking havoc on
the DNA of Wasteland survivors makes them

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:07.520
as “impure” as the synths created with
“untainted” DNA from Sean.

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:10.680
The second myth is about human nature and
morality.

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:15.590
Maxson argues against Danse’s humanity on
the grounds that: “Those ethics that it’s

00:07:15.590 --> 00:07:17.629
striving to champion aren’t even its own.

00:07:17.629 --> 00:07:21.690
They were artificially inserted in an attempt
to have it blend into society.”

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:25.819
For many humanists, there’s either a human
nature which makes us innately good, or at

00:07:25.819 --> 00:07:30.080
the very least, a free will which enables
the possibility of ethics: unlike animals

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:32.729
or machines, we can “choose” to be virtuous.

00:07:32.729 --> 00:07:37.720
Maxson criticizes Danse for having “artificially
inserted ethics”, the irony of this, coming

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:42.120
from a man raised in a heavily regimented
military organization where higher powers

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:46.240
dictate a code of ethics to a set of subordinates,
seems to be lost on him.

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:51.180
"If you intend to stay within our ranks, you need to obey our tennents without question."

00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:55.980
For Haraway, our entire being is crafted by
the cultures, societies, and environment that

00:07:55.990 --> 00:07:57.520
we come into contact with.

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:01.240
For instance, exploring Kellog’s memories,
and his traumatic upbringing, can tell us

00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:04.530
as much about who he is as his cybernetic
implants.

00:08:04.530 --> 00:08:08.560
If the Institute inserted a set of ethics
in Danse, is it all that different from the

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:12.720
ethics instilled in us by our parents, our
church, or our schools?

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:17.120
Technology invading human nature is a constant
fear in real society - and constitutes the

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:19.389
third myth espoused by the Brotherhood.

00:08:19.389 --> 00:08:23.129
Using tools is what allowed us to evolve into
who we are today, and the technologies we

00:08:23.129 --> 00:08:27.669
developed along the way like agriculture,
architecture, and manufacturing shaped us

00:08:27.669 --> 00:08:30.270
as much as we shaped the world with them.

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:34.250
Before the synths, the Brotherhood manifested
this fear in their distrust of technology:

00:08:34.250 --> 00:08:38.560
believing the pursuit of science to be a reckless
transcending of human limitations and control.

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:50.460
Yet, at no point does it register for them
that flying around a blimp stocked full of

00:08:50.470 --> 00:08:53.280
nukes might also be an act of hubris.

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:57.480
The figure of the synth creates a rupture
in our understanding of the human, complicating

00:08:57.480 --> 00:08:58.480
these myths.

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:02.640
For Haraway, this is a good thing: our rigid
conception of “human” is what allows people

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:07.810
to ostracize ghouls and enslave synths in
Fallout, and has justified centuries of oppression

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:09.450
against racialized others.

00:09:12.980 --> 00:09:17.870
Haraway argues for a “cyborg politics”
one that rejoices “in the illegitimate fusion

00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:19.320
of animal and machine.”

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:22.900
In other words, instead of denying that we
are cyborgs, we should embrace it.

00:09:22.900 --> 00:09:27.760
We are not only the fusion of flesh and machine,
but society, history and culture.

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:32.450
The Institute, even if accidentally, manages
to confront us with this pollution of “the human."

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:36.850
Father leaves you a synthetic version of himself
for you to raise as your own child.

00:09:36.850 --> 00:09:42.880
The Synths, who we’re told are mere automatons,
bristle for freedom regardless of their categorization.

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:47.520
Even the fact that Synth’s are built using
actual human DNA further blurs these boundaries.

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:50.840
Valentine, who is aware he is living with
somebody else’s memories planted into his

00:09:50.840 --> 00:09:55.740
artificial mind, struggles with what it means
“to be” knowing that he, and his past,

00:09:55.740 --> 00:09:57.000
are fabrications.

00:10:21.860 --> 00:10:25.140
And there’s this 
speech from Danse: “It’s true, I was built

00:10:25.150 --> 00:10:29.700
within the confines of a laboratory, and some
of my memories aren’t my own.

00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:34.730
But when I saw my brothers dying at my feet,
I felt sorrow.

00:10:34.730 --> 00:10:37.860
When I defeated an enemy of the Brotherhood,
I felt pride.

00:10:37.900 --> 00:10:39.620
Don’t you understand?

00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:41.660
I thought I was human, Arthur.”

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:45.920
Perhaps the most interesting lesson we can
draw from Fallout 4 comes from the story of

00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:46.920
Pinocchio.

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:50.130
In Pinocchio, a wooden marionette strives
to become fully human.

00:10:50.130 --> 00:10:54.330
It’s only when he learns the human traits
of selflessness and honesty that he is finally

00:10:54.330 --> 00:10:55.580
transformed.

00:10:55.580 --> 00:10:59.620
Fallout offers us a few parallels: there’s
Curie, who wants to become a real human,

00:11:03.740 --> 00:11:06.150
but settles for her brain being downloaded into
a synth.

00:11:06.150 --> 00:11:10.260
Then there’s the relationship between Father
and the synth Shaun he built, which somewhat

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:12.900
parallels the relationship between Gepetto
and Pinnochio.

00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:22.140
Both Danse and Valentine ground their claims
in humanity, like the story of Pinocchio,

00:11:22.150 --> 00:11:23.700
in their human values.

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:27.230
And what better metaphor for what it means
to be human than a puppet who only becomes

00:11:27.230 --> 00:11:31.710
a human after he learns to obey his father
and accept society’s rules?

